WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.390
Speaker 1: Today we're going to be speaking
with Gary Stevenson, who's an inequality economist

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:06.720
and former interest rates trader.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.400
Gary, really great having you on.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.400
I appreciate your time.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.110
Speaker 4: Thanks for having me.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:15.841
Speaker 1: So, I mean, maybe just to start
with a little bit about your background, interest

00:00:15.841 --> 00:00:18.200
rates trading, how how does that work?

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.690
And with whom are you trading?

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:20.690
Speaker 4: Okay.

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:28.290
So I worked as an interest rates trader from
2008 to 2014 at Citibank, just here in a skyscraper

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.660
off to my left.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.989
2008, you probably remember we the year of
the Great Financial Crisis.

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:35.989
Yes.

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:38.589
At that time, all the interest rates in the
whole world went to zero.

00:00:38.589 --> 00:00:43.289
So as an interest rates trade, the my job
was basically to bet on when those interest

00:00:43.289 --> 00:00:49.960
rates come back up again, which is kind of
a proxy for when will the economy recover.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.059
You expect that when the economy recovers,
we get demand coming back and rates start

00:00:53.059 --> 00:00:54.329
to come back up.

00:00:54.329 --> 00:01:01.069
What's interesting about that is every single
year from 2008 to 2020, markets predicted

00:01:01.069 --> 00:01:05.979
that rates would be normalized the following
year, which was wrong almost every single

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:06.979
year.

00:01:06.979 --> 00:01:10.360
So I made a lot money by betting that they
would stay zero for a long time.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:18.009
Speaker 1: One of the criticisms that many
will make about that, that type of industry

00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:25.600
is that it is not economically stimulative
in the way that making shoes and selling shoes

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.310
to someone who then then wears them is right.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:33.539
This is a common criticism that we hear about
derivatives trading and financial markets,

00:01:33.539 --> 00:01:36.710
Wall Street, etc., having been inside of it.

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:41.510
How do you feel about that claim that trading
interest rates compared to, for example, running

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:46.869
a restaurant or a dry cleaning business or
manufacturing eyeglasses, are fundamentally

00:01:46.869 --> 00:01:49.320
different in terms of what they do for the
economy.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.270
Speaker 4: Yeah, listen, you won't get much
argument from me.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:56.499
I'm afraid I didn't go into that industry
to be economically productive or stimulative.

00:01:56.499 --> 00:02:00.850
I went to industry because I was really poor
and I didn't want to be poor anymore.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:01.850
Right.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:03.130
And from that perspective, it worked.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:08.600
What I would say is, you know, I've worked
in economics for a long time now in a lot

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:09.600
of different spaces.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.850
I've worked in finance.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:14.730
I've got a master's degree from Oxford University
here in England.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:17.770
I've been in media talking about economics
for a while now.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:21.640
I've been sort of bouncing around sort of
activism, think tanks.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.010
And what I would say is.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:29.010
The markets are the only place where somebody
like me can be the number one guy.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.540
Within a couple of years.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.300
Because if you're really, really good and
you're right.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:33.600
Year after year, you get noticed.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:39.540
And, you know, I put an Oracle up two years
ago at the beginning of Kobe saying that we're

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:44.160
going to see at the end of Kobe a massive
crisis of inequality, of inflation, of cost,

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.360
of living.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.750
And I put a video on YouTube saying the same
thing.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:50.370
Those predictions were exactly correct.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.020
Now, I did the same thing when I was working
as a trader.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:55.080
And within a year, two years, you're famous.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.940
But in this world, if you're right, people
are not really risen to the top.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:05.910
So what I would say is trading is an interesting
space because a younger pullback can get famous

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:06.910
by being right.

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:11.300
In economics, I mean, there's not that many
spaces where you can do that outside of that.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.330
Speaker 1: So you mentioned inequality.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.730
I want to focus in on that a little bit.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:21.680
You know, sometimes discussions about inequality,
particularly in in the U.K., in the United

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:27.980
States, the conversations become sort of like
ethical and moral conversations.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:35.560
Does it does a wealthy country have a responsibility
or a duty to provide some bottom, some floor

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.540
to standard of living?

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.600
And who has earned what or who deserves what?

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:44.290
That's one side of the discussion, and different
people might have different feelings about

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:45.290
that.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:51.230
I've also argued from an economic and financial
standpoint that there should be an incentive

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:57.510
for the wealthy to also guarantee some minimum
standard of living and reduce inequality.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:02.760
Because it's also, if you consider a sort
of demand side stimulus approach, it's also

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.610
good for the rich people, it's good for the
businesses.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:09.080
It's good to have more potential customers
who can afford whatever it is that you're

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:10.080
selling.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.840
And having reduced inequality reduces crime.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.459
It reduces a lot of other costs.

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:22.290
Now, I would still make a moral and ethical
case as well, but I want to explore maybe

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.730
the financial and economic case to be made.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:28.380
Does that track with your beliefs about the
damaging reality of inequality?

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:29.430
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:30.470
Speaker 4: 100%.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.230
I mean, I don't come from a political family.

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:34.440
I didn't want to be an activist.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:39.060
I didn't want to be a political YouTuber,
which, in a sense, is what I've become.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.060
Mm hmm.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:44.480
But my job was to bet on the future of the
global economy.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:50.370
And I worked in an industry where every single
year, the markets as a whole said next year,

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:51.480
things will get better.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:54.930
And for the first three years I worked the
industry, things didn't get better.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:57.330
And, you know, this is a problem, right?

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:02.360
Like, if you if you have a doctor who says
every patient is going to get better and the

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.060
patients keep dying, you've got a problem.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:05.060
Right?

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:08.260
Because these guys, the economists and it's
not just traders, it's also guys in central

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:12.120
banks, governments in academia, they keep
saying things are going to get better and

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.200
things don't get better.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.200
Right.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:15.200
So it's clearly a fundamental problem.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.949
Then for me, you know, I thought a lot about
what what is the reason why we're not getting

00:05:19.949 --> 00:05:20.949
the recovery.

00:05:20.949 --> 00:05:22.940
Basically, what is the fundamental reason?

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:27.780
I think I think actually one of the reasons
why I was able to be such a successful trader

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:31.990
and at one point I was Citibank's most profitable
trader in the world is because I actually

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:35.510
am one of the few traders who comes from a
very poor background.

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:39.900
So in that after the 2008 financial crisis,
we weren't getting that recovery.

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.630
We had to ask this question of why aren't
people spending more money?

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:46.800
Well, I was one of the few people who could
go and ask my friends and family, ordinary

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.740
people, why are they spending more money?

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.160
And ask people now.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.340
Nine times out of ten, you'll get the same
answer because we don't have any more money.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:55.340
Speaker 1: Right.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:06:00.270
Speaker 4: And if the vast majority of people
don't have any extra money to spend, you are

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:02.570
going to have economic crises of demand.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:05.430
And that's what we've been for, you know,
15 years, essentially.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:10.910
So for me, this is and of course, you're 100%
right when you say that there's a moral, ethical

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:12.290
argument here.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:14.780
But there's also 100% plain economic argument.

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:19.340
And I'm not just saying this memo standpoint,
I've made millions of pounds betting on this.

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:23.870
If you do not deal with inequality is inevitable
that your economy will decline and decline

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:24.919
over time.

00:06:24.919 --> 00:06:32.620
Speaker 1: I'm curious, do you have a sense
of the prevailing political views among the

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:34.220
people that were your fellow traders?

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:39.919
I mean, was was it a sort of supply side right
leaning group of people were.

00:06:39.919 --> 00:06:41.160
What was it like?

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:46.300
Speaker 4: So trading is an elite profession
in the sense that it is one of the best paid

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:47.300
professions.

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:48.660
You can you can get into it.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:54.000
So for that reason and nowadays very, very
few people get in who are not from originally

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:55.580
pretty wealthy backgrounds.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:59.500
So you're getting people coming in generally
who are quite wealthy.

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:00.860
A lot of them have got economics degrees.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:06.210
And I think when someone has an economic theory,
they tend to have kind of absorbed into themselves

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:10.770
some certain ideas which which you might describe
as quite right wing ideas.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:16.970
But at the same time and what I think is interesting
about trading is these guys are very, very

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:20.720
strongly rewarded for being right and harshly
punished for being wrong.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:26.740
So I think especially people who I worked
with, you know, I've said it here, I was one

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:29.110
of the most productive traders at the bank
and I was very vocal about it.

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:32.370
I always said, look, we are never going to
get an economic recovery because of a crisis

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:33.370
of inequality, you know?

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:36.430
And I was saying that in 2010, 2011.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:40.580
So obviously, people who are working with
me, then they see that I've been right for

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:42.430
ten, 11, 12 years.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:44.610
It's in that world.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:46.870
The main thing is that you have to be right.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:52.850
So I think people who I've worked with tend
to, you know, eventually they see these guys

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:55.380
want to make money and they see the guy who's
right and that's me.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:59.200
So they come around to my point of view, which
is, okay, inequality is killing the economy.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:02.330
The problem is inequality is not killing the
economy for everyone, right?

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:04.650
For rich people, inequality is absolutely
fantastic.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:08.860
And these guys are making half a million pounds
a year, £1,000,000 a year.

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:13.389
So as much as they can see that there's a
disaster happening, they're not the best incentivized

00:08:13.389 --> 00:08:14.389
to fix it.

00:08:14.389 --> 00:08:22.290
Speaker 1: When it comes to reducing inequality,
there's a lot of different ideas and approaches.

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:26.139
They're often very much tied in to one's prevailing
political beliefs.

00:08:26.139 --> 00:08:31.470
And it's not just a question of what does
the economics necessarily show?

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:35.409
What sort of framework or approach do you
think would be the most effective?

00:08:35.409 --> 00:08:40.349
Speaker 4: So I think there's there's basically
two two ways to answer that question.

00:08:40.349 --> 00:08:43.939
One is what would be taking the most effective
way to reduce inequality?

00:08:43.939 --> 00:08:45.490
And one is what is politically achievable?

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:49.060
Because this is a really like hot political
conversation right now.

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:52.850
The thing which I generally tend to advocate
for is a wealth tax, because I think that

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:55.870
is the one which will most likely to get people
on board with.

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:58.629
But, you know, there are loads of ways you
can reduce inequality.

00:08:58.629 --> 00:09:03.269
You know, you could, for example, bring in
you could encourage or force the rich to spend

00:09:03.269 --> 00:09:07.060
much, much, much more money which would drive
wages up and give us an economic boom.

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:08.980
You know, this would be effective as well.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:11.790
But I think what's important to understand
is.

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:15.360
In the absence of intervention, inequality
rises super, super quickly.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:18.560
And this is like a very simple thing, which
I think people need to understand.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:21.600
When people have high amounts of wealth, that
wealth makes wealth.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.809
So if somebody has, say, $100 million, they're
going to make in an average year, three $5

00:09:26.809 --> 00:09:28.680
million from that money.

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:31.040
So these guys will get much, much richer over
time.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:34.350
And, you know, in the first year of COVID,
I think the first two years, the average billionaire

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.290
doubled their wealth in the U.S.

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:39.430
So these guys will get richer if we don't
do anything.

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:41.360
So I think.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:44.860
It's really a case of how do you prevent the
crisis from worsening.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:46.949
But I think.

00:09:46.949 --> 00:09:49.819
You have to look at the tax system because
at the moment we have a tax system where I

00:09:49.819 --> 00:09:51.079
come from a poor background.

00:09:51.079 --> 00:09:52.079
I made millions of dollars.

00:09:52.079 --> 00:09:53.459
I paid 50%.

00:09:53.459 --> 00:09:56.170
But if you come from a wealthy background
and you have money in the family, you can

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:57.170
pay nothing.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:09:58.689
And you can be much, much richer than I am.

00:09:58.689 --> 00:10:02.769
So if you have a system where the super rich
pay nothing and people like me pay 50% inequality

00:10:02.769 --> 00:10:03.769
and never going to rise.

00:10:03.769 --> 00:10:04.980
Speaker 1: Right.

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:11.839
And this is where you start to get into resistance,
where you will hear sort of like prepackaged

00:10:11.839 --> 00:10:12.839
talking points.

00:10:12.839 --> 00:10:20.600
Like, for example, if you do that, you will
kill innovation and the job creating wealthy

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:23.180
will not create jobs.

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:29.339
You know, when I look at it, I think to myself
the cost of hoarding the money has to be high.

00:10:29.339 --> 00:10:34.350
That's how you incentivize people to do something
more productive with the money, make it expensive

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:39.079
to just sit and earn the interest, which sounds
sort of like what you're saying here.

00:10:39.079 --> 00:10:41.559
Speaker 4: But look, there's two ways.

00:10:41.559 --> 00:10:43.279
There's two things that you need to grow the
economy.

00:10:43.279 --> 00:10:44.279
Yeah, of course.

00:10:44.279 --> 00:10:48.999
You need people making investments so that
you can have those productive investments

00:10:48.999 --> 00:10:50.699
in growth, but you also need demand.

00:10:50.699 --> 00:10:52.980
Look, I'm a wealthy person, okay?

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:54.119
I live off my investments.

00:10:54.119 --> 00:10:55.769
I live off my investment income.

00:10:55.769 --> 00:11:00.470
It is very, very hard to make productive,
real investments in this economy.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:02.610
And, you know, we've had.

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:06.889
1213 years of zero interest rates, effectively
negative real interest rates.

00:11:06.889 --> 00:11:08.910
Interest rates still are aggressively negative,
right?

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:13.790
What that means is if you are a rich person,
you cannot get any interest on your money.

00:11:13.790 --> 00:11:17.050
So there should be huge amounts of money flooding
into investments.

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:18.240
But why aren't it?

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.269
It's because the customers are not that there's
not enough customers to create that real growth.

00:11:22.269 --> 00:11:23.269
You need two things.

00:11:23.269 --> 00:11:24.779
You need two sides of the coin.

00:11:24.779 --> 00:11:28.790
You need customers who can spend and you need
people who can save and invest.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:32.860
But when inequality gets too high, then all
of that money is flowing to the super rich

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:34.290
who are trying to invest it.

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:36.410
But there are no customers at the end to spend.

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:38.230
And you see these crises at the moment.

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:42.759
And I think when we're in a situation of 15
years negative interest rates, these supply

00:11:42.759 --> 00:11:44.449
side arguments have to be dead.

00:11:44.449 --> 00:11:45.870
They have to be dead.

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:49.639
You would not get 15 years of negative interest
rates if the problem was not enough people

00:11:49.639 --> 00:11:50.790
investing their money.

00:11:50.790 --> 00:11:56.189
Speaker 1: This next question may be tough
because the current tax rates are different

00:11:56.189 --> 00:11:57.249
in different countries.

00:11:57.249 --> 00:12:01.610
And, you know, here in the United States,
we have a progressive income tax and then

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:05.889
we also have capital gains and there's short
term capital gains and long term capital gains.

00:12:05.889 --> 00:12:11.170
And one movement is, well, tax capital gains
as income taxes, which would be much higher.

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:17.740
This would be a way to make it to better incentivize
the rich to spend and put that money into

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:20.249
something other than just collecting the gains.

00:12:20.249 --> 00:12:24.449
The tax rates are different in the U.K. they're
different in all these different countries.

00:12:24.449 --> 00:12:28.680
Do you have some sense of what a more realistic
framework should be about?

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:35.519
Two things one, where income tax rates should
be, but maybe just as importantly, capital

00:12:35.519 --> 00:12:40.829
gains, because capital gains rates being lower
is often a way for the wealthy to avoid income

00:12:40.829 --> 00:12:41.860
taxes altogether.

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:45.560
Speaker 4: So I think there's two things to
think about.

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:46.779
One is the rates.

00:12:46.779 --> 00:12:49.689
And one is avoidance, largely legal avoidance.

00:12:49.689 --> 00:12:50.689
Yes.

00:12:50.689 --> 00:12:52.959
I think one thing that needs to be recognized
about capital gains is you pay capital gains

00:12:52.959 --> 00:12:53.959
when you sell.

00:12:53.959 --> 00:12:54.959
Speaker 1: Correct.

00:12:54.959 --> 00:12:58.860
Speaker 4: So imagine you have a wealth of
$100 million, which means you're making three

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:01.579
$5 million income a year on your wealth.

00:13:01.579 --> 00:13:03.269
Are you going to sell?

00:13:03.269 --> 00:13:07.779
Well, you're never going to have to sell while
you're making $35 million a year income.

00:13:07.779 --> 00:13:09.750
So rich people never have to sell.

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:11.110
If you're super rich, you never have to sell.

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:14.459
So the first thing is so you can bring the
rate up to whatever you want.

00:13:14.459 --> 00:13:15.999
You know, these guys never sell.

00:13:15.999 --> 00:13:16.999
They're never going to pay.

00:13:16.999 --> 00:13:21.749
And there is a movement to say, okay, well,
we need to tax unrealized gains.

00:13:21.749 --> 00:13:23.470
The most important thing is it needs to work.

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:27.389
It needs to work in the sense of it needs
to effectively tax the very richest in society.

00:13:27.389 --> 00:13:28.389
Right.

00:13:28.389 --> 00:13:30.489
Because I know there are people in the middle
that are worried you're going to try and tax

00:13:30.489 --> 00:13:33.440
super rich, they're going to avoid it and
then it's going to fall on me.

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:35.290
And if that is what happens, then it will
be a failure.

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:39.689
So this needs to be rigorously tested against
does it work on the very, very rich, the richest,

00:13:39.689 --> 00:13:40.689
you know.

00:13:40.689 --> 00:13:41.689
Is Jeff Bezos going to pay?

00:13:41.689 --> 00:13:42.809
Is it going to pay?

00:13:42.809 --> 00:13:45.899
Are they going to continue to pay very little
tax on their wealth?

00:13:45.899 --> 00:13:51.690
So raising tax on capital gains, raising the
rate is one thing, but you need to get rid

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:52.690
of evasion.

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:57.230
I think really what you need to look at is
what is somebodys income over the course of

00:13:57.230 --> 00:13:59.549
their whole life and how much tax they pay.

00:13:59.549 --> 00:14:00.959
That should be the measure.

00:14:00.959 --> 00:14:04.329
How much wealth is this guy have and how much
tax is he paid?

00:14:04.329 --> 00:14:09.429
And if we're talking about numbers of less
than less than 30, 20% for people who are

00:14:09.429 --> 00:14:11.410
billionaires, you've messed up and you need
to fix it.

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:12.410
Speaker 1: Right.

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:18.949
Is there one particular mechanism that you
have found is most resistant to avoidance?

00:14:18.949 --> 00:14:26.480
Speaker 4: To be honest.

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:30.089
The problem is often these taxes are brought
in with a void.

00:14:30.089 --> 00:14:35.540
It's kind of in mine because you have to consider
the game the politicians are playing.

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:39.179
On the one hand, most ordinary people actually
want the rich to pay higher taxes.

00:14:39.179 --> 00:14:42.560
They think it's fair that the rich pay lower
taxes than the rest of us.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:46.579
They want higher taxes, but the political
parties are funded by super rich people who

00:14:46.579 --> 00:14:47.929
don't want to pay taxes.

00:14:47.929 --> 00:14:50.459
So how can you make both of these groups right.

00:14:50.459 --> 00:14:54.459
You can do is you can bring in a tax that's
called a wealth tax, but you can put loopholes

00:14:54.459 --> 00:14:57.489
in it such that the super rich can avoid it
and then everybody wins.

00:14:57.489 --> 00:14:58.489
Right.

00:14:58.489 --> 00:14:59.739
You can say to ordinary people, we brought
the tax in.

00:14:59.739 --> 00:15:01.369
And you can say to the rich, but it doesn't
matter because you have to pay it.

00:15:01.369 --> 00:15:02.369
Right.

00:15:02.369 --> 00:15:04.999
So I think there's a real problem here of
politicians.

00:15:04.999 --> 00:15:10.049
You know, often they themselves are super
wealthy or become super wealthy after leaving

00:15:10.049 --> 00:15:11.049
office.

00:15:11.049 --> 00:15:13.420
And they're not well incentivized to bring
in a system that works.

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:15.970
I think, you know, anything could work, really.

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:17.620
A capital gains system could work.

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:19.089
An inheritance tax system could work.

00:15:19.089 --> 00:15:21.839
I've got ideas about putting wealth into a
time limit.

00:15:21.839 --> 00:15:22.839
Trust such.

00:15:22.839 --> 00:15:24.769
You can only give it your kids for a certain
amount of time.

00:15:24.769 --> 00:15:29.749
All of these things could work, but they will
only work with serious political will behind

00:15:29.749 --> 00:15:31.100
them to actually make them effective.

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:35.069
Because at the moment the incentivisation
of politicians is to pretend to tax the rich,

00:15:35.069 --> 00:15:36.839
but actually not tax the rich.

00:15:36.839 --> 00:15:40.249
And that's what a lot of the work I do is
talking directly to ordinary people, because

00:15:40.249 --> 00:15:43.279
these are the guys who have been hurt by the
system we currently have.

00:15:43.279 --> 00:15:47.480
Speaker 1: Yeah, a depressing but accurate
note to end on, I guess I would say.

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:51.519
Gary Stevenson, inequality economist, former
interest rates trader.

00:15:51.519 --> 00:15:53.299
Really appreciate your time and insights today,
Gary.

00:15:53.299 --> 00:15:54.019
Speaker 4: Thanks, Dave.

